      "id": 39,
      "output": "To determine how many miles Dana can travel in six hours, we first need to calculate her speeds:\n\n1. **Running Speed**: Since skipping is half as fast as running, and skipping is given as 3 miles per hour, running must be \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) miles per hour.\n   \n2. **Walking Speed**: Given that running is four times faster than walking, walking speed is \\(6 / 4 = 1.5\\) miles per hour.\n\nNext, we break down the six-hour period into parts:\n- One-third of the time (which is 2 hours) will be spent running.\n- Two-thirds of the time (which is also 2 hours) will be spent walking.\n\n**Calculating Distance Traveled During Running**:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Distance}_{\\text{running}} = 6 \\text{ mph} \\times 2 \\text{ hours} = 12 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\n**Calculating Distance Traveled During Walking**:\n\\[ \\text{Distance}_{\\text{walking}} = 1.5 \\text{ mph} \\times 2 \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nFinally, adding these distances together gives us the total distance traveled in six hours:\n\\[ \\text{Total Distance} = \\text{Distance}_{\\text{running}} + \\text{Distance}_{\\text{walking}} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total Distance} = 12 \\text{ miles} + 3 \\text{ miles} = 15 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is 15.",
